WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.320
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:10.080
Today we are going to see progress&nbsp;
in text to image research that is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:17.280
incredible it hardly seems believable. So,&nbsp;
what is text to image? Simple - these are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:24.420
AI-based techniques where a text prompt from&nbsp;
us goes in, and a beautiful image comes out.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:30.660
There are already a large set of techniques&nbsp;
that can perform text to image really well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:38.520
for instance, OpenAI’s DALL-E 2 can do it, where&nbsp;
we wait approximately 10 seconds for each image.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:45.360
Or, we can even run it on our own hardware&nbsp;
with the free and open source Stable Diffusion.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:53.460
So, are we done? What else is there to invent&nbsp;
here? Why write more papers? Well, by the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.460 --> 00:01:00.300
you finish this video, I hope you will agree with&nbsp;
me that the only possible answer is: my goodness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:06.060
there is so much to be done and so&nbsp;
much that has just been improved.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:13.560
For instance, this new technique from Google that&nbsp;
they call Muse can perform mask-free editing too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:20.340
What is that? To be able to appreciate what&nbsp;
that is, let’s look at what mask-based editing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:27.240
looks like. Imagine that we have a wonderful&nbsp;
photo here, but we would like to change the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:33.720
location of the background. No matter, let’s just&nbsp;
highlight this region, which we will call a mask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:41.580
and ask for a different background. And, yes, it&nbsp;
can repaint the image as if it were in New York,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:51.060
Paris, or San Francisco. Wonderful. This is image&nbsp;
inpainting by using a mask and a text prompt.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:58.980
And now, let’s do this, but without a mask!&nbsp;
What? How is this even possible? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:05.880
we can tell the AI, come on, you are smart enough&nbsp;
to know where these objects are and what they are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:12.600
so, you do the masking yourself.&nbsp;
Automatically. So, can it? Let’s see.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:19.680
There is a cake in this image, and a&nbsp;
coffee latte. But, psst! Don’t tell the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:27.000
AI where it is. Confidential information. Let it&nbsp;
find out itself. Now, little AI, please change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:34.440
the cake to a croissant, if it even exists,&nbsp;
who knows, the latte art should form a flower.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:45.000
And… wow. Look at that. Who could say that this&nbsp;
was not the original image? It has done absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.020
amazing. And, I am a light transport researcher&nbsp;
by trade, so I cannot resist mentioning that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:57.780
the specular highlights on the new plate&nbsp;
are also excellent. Good job, little AI!

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:04.440
But its mask free editing capabilities&nbsp;
can do even better. Look. We can change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:11.880
our clothes super easily, even with text,&nbsp;
remember, synthesizing text properly was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:17.760
quite difficult for previous techniques,&nbsp;
and this just does it easily. So good!

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:25.380
Now, get this, because we can also do this with&nbsp;
drawings, and if we do, something amazing happens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:32.460
Look. We can start out from a crude drawing&nbsp;
of a cat, and ask it to morph into different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:41.100
other animals. And through this, it can become a&nbsp;
dog, or a pig, a raccoon, or even other animals.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:47.520
This is possible because this new technique is&nbsp;
not your usual diffusion-based process like many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:54.780
previous image generators. What does that mean?&nbsp;
It means that it does not start out from noise and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.780 --> 00:04:01.680
does not reorganize this noise to get a coherent&nbsp;
image. It does not think in terms of noise at all.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:09.960
But, we are not done yet, not even close! It&nbsp;
can also perform image outpainting. That is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:16.560
taking this part of the image and replacing&nbsp;
the entirety of the image around it using a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:22.440
text prompt. Travel around the world&nbsp;
with just one text prompt. So cool!

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:28.800
Now, have a look at these images and&nbsp;
their prompts. Great works, right? But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:34.920
there is something that ties them together. Do&nbsp;
you know what? Well, hold on to your papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:40.380
because all of these images took just&nbsp;
approximately one second to generate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:46.260
That’s right, this is up to 10 times&nbsp;
faster than previous techniques! And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:52.740
all this less than a year after DALL-E&nbsp;
2 has been published. That is insanity.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:58.500
And it can also perform things that other&nbsp;
previous techniques had a great deal of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:06.780
trouble with. What are those? Well, two examples,&nbsp;
cardinality and composition. Cardinality means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:12.540
that if we ask for three elephants standing&nbsp;
on top of each other, we really get three.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:19.980
If we ask for four bottles of wine, we get&nbsp;
four. And if we ask for 10 bottles of wine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:26.040
wait a minute…yes, apparently not&nbsp;
even this technique is perfect.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:33.120
It also does well when it comes to composition.&nbsp;
If we ask for the two baseballs to be to the left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:39.240
of the tennis balls, the AI understands that&nbsp;
and thus, they will likely end up being there.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:45.780
However, I also love how it combines all of&nbsp;
these these concepts together. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:53.220
here, we can do mask-free editing while keeping&nbsp;
the composition of the original image the same.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:59.940
This way, we can transform our cat into&nbsp;
a dog, change a small basketball into an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:07.860
American football, or make our cat yawn, or even&nbsp;
change these flowers. And note that we did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:12.480
need a mask for this, no highlighting&nbsp;
regions where the cats and roses are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:19.920
we just write what we want and the AI does it!&nbsp;
Also note once again that the composition of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:27.300
original image remains intact. I absolutely&nbsp;
love this. Such an amazing tool, and now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:33.600
a really fast one too. One second for&nbsp;
each of these? Sign me up right now!

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:39.900
And just imagine that two more papers down&nbsp;
the line perhaps all this will be possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:46.860
to do in real time. We might be able to create&nbsp;
little virtual worlds with the speed of thought.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:50.880
How cool is that! What a time to be alive!

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:56.700
So, what do you think? What would you use&nbsp;
this for? Let me know in the comments below!

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:59.940
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

